From 775c92d03e850905cdb0c261a4aa9ad2c552f9ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: embedded-lnd <embedded-lnd@lqwd.local>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 17:35:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] security: add an on-chain destination allowlist and an RPC
 deny list

Two hardening controls for an appliance-style node, both enforced by a pair
of gRPC interceptors installed as the innermost interceptors in the chain,
and both configured in a new [security] section of lnd.conf.

  security.onchain-allowlist   the only on-chain destinations this node may
                               pay. Checked on SendCoins, SendMany, the
                               CloseChannel delivery address, the OpenChannel
                               and BatchOpenChannel close address, and the
                               WalletKit SendOutputs scripts.

  security.disable-rpc         gRPC methods that are refused for every caller.
                               Fully qualified names, or a whole service with
                               a /service/* wildcard.

Neither is a macaroon permission. They cannot be lifted by baking a new
macaroon and they apply to the admin macaroon too, which is the case they
exist for: an appliance whose credentials are assumed to be reachable by
whoever holds the box.

Being the innermost interceptors also means the policy sees each request
exactly as the handler will, so a registered RPC middleware that rewrites
requests cannot be used to slip a different destination past the allowlist.

Some RPCs can build, sign or broadcast a transaction without ever naming a
destination in the request - PSBT funding and signing, PublishTransaction,
SubmitPackage, SignOutputRaw, the MuSig2 signing session, FundingStateStep.
There is nothing in them for the allowlist to inspect, so while an allowlist
is in force they are denied outright, and reopening them takes an explicit
security.allow-unsafe-rpc.

Bad policies fail at startup rather than at the first call: an address for
the wrong network, a deny list entry that is not a method name, a deny list
entry naming a method this build does not serve, or an upfront-shutdown-address
that is not itself an allowed destination.
